# Normalise line endings in the repository.
#
# This repo is developed on Windows (`core.autocrlf=true`) and deployed to
# Ubuntu. Without this file, a single editor save can commit CRLF into a shell
# script, and the VPS then fails it with a message that names the wrong problem:
#
#   /usr/bin/env: 'bash\r': No such file or directory
#
# `* text=auto` normalises to LF in the repository. The explicit `eol=lf` rules
# below go further and force LF *in the working tree too*, for the files where
# a CRLF would break something on the server no matter what git thinks.

* text=auto

# Executed by the shell on Linux — a trailing \r breaks the shebang.
*.sh            text eol=lf

# Parsed by nginx and systemd, both of which treat \r as part of the value.
*.conf          text eol=lf
*.service       text eol=lf

# Read by the Prisma CLI and by dotenv; \r ends up inside DATABASE_URL.
*.prisma        text eol=lf
.env.example    text eol=lf

# Parsed by Papa Parse; \r in the final column of a header row would break the
# alias matching in lib/parseLeadsCsv.ts.
*.csv           text eol=lf

# Binary — never touch.
*.ico           binary
*.png           binary
*.jpg           binary
*.woff          binary
*.woff2         binary
